logo.js
Purpose

logo.js controls the interactive personal logo:

</>

The logo should appear near the beginning of the webpage as a visually prominent object with a metallic appearance.

It should have a sense of physical presence through lighting, shading, subtle movement, and interaction.

The user can interact with it by clicking it. When clicked, the logo produces the sound of a metal object being struck using an audio file stored locally in the project.

The overall impression should be:

A physical metallic </> object embedded inside a digital environment.

It should feel tactile without becoming a game-like UI element.

What this module owns

logo.js handles:

1. Logo initialization
2. Logo element discovery/creation
3. Logo entrance animation
4. Interactive hover behavior
5. Pointer-based lighting / subtle movement
6. Click interaction
7. Metallic strike audio playback
8. Audio cooldown / interaction protection
9. Optional click animation
10. Accessibility state
11. Cleanup

It does NOT handle:

1. Background cosmic particles
2. Terminal functionality
3. Page navigation
4. Blog functionality
5. Education animation
6. Global colors/theme
7. General typography
8. General page layout
Logo representation

The canonical logo is:

</>

The actual implementation should preferably use normal text or an SVG rather than an image.

For example:

<div id="personal-logo" class="personal-logo">
    &lt;/&gt;
</div>

or an SVG representation if you eventually want much finer control over the geometry.

The important thing is that the visual identity remains exactly:

</>
Metallic appearance

The JavaScript should not manually draw the metal appearance.

That belongs primarily to:

logo-animation.css

CSS can provide:

metallic gradient
highlight
shadow
specular reflection
depth
subtle bevel
glow

The JS merely controls the dynamic aspects.

Think:

CSS
 ↓
physical material

JS
 ↓
physical interaction

This keeps the module clean.

Initial entrance animation

When the webpage loads, the logo should already be conceptually present, but it should enter into the scene.

Possible sequence:

page begins
    ↓
logo appears
    ↓
slight opacity increase
    ↓
slight vertical movement
    ↓
subtle settling
    ↓
logo becomes stationary

The animation should feel like an object being placed into the scene rather than a flashy website intro.

Avoid:

spin
explosion
huge zoom
neon flash

A subtle "settling into reality" animation fits the concept much better.

Idle state

After the entrance animation completes, the logo should mostly remain still.

You can give it an extremely subtle idle effect:

tiny light movement
tiny floating movement
very subtle reflection

But don't make it continuously bounce.

The logo should feel heavy.

That weight is important to the metallic/physical illusion.

Pointer interaction

When the cursor approaches the logo, the logo can respond subtly.

Possible effects:

cursor approaches
       ↓
specular highlight shifts
       ↓
tiny positional response
       ↓
slight depth/shadow change

The effect should be based on the cursor's position relative to the logo.

Conceptually:

cursor
   ↓
distance + direction
   ↓
interaction strength
   ↓
light/shadow/transform

The closer the cursor is, the stronger the response.

Don't make it follow the cursor

The logo should NOT behave like:

cursor moves right
       ↓
logo moves right

That would make it feel like a UI widget.

Instead, the logo should behave more like an object being observed under a moving light source.

For example:

cursor → upper-left
        ↓
highlight shifts upper-left
shadow subtly changes

cursor → lower-right
        ↓
highlight shifts lower-right
shadow subtly changes

That is what sells the material.

Click interaction

Clicking the logo triggers a small physical response.

Conceptually:

click
 ↓
logo receives impact
 ↓
tiny compression / displacement
 ↓
metal strike sound
 ↓
logo settles back

The visual response should be extremely short.

Something like:

normal
   ↓
impact
   ↓
tiny vibration
   ↓
settle
   ↓
normal

No giant animation.

The sound should be the main feedback.

Audio

The metal strike sound is stored locally in the project.

For example:

assets/
└── audio/
    └── metal-strike.mp3

The exact filename/path should be configurable rather than hardcoded throughout the module.

For example:

audioSrc: 'assets/audio/metal-strike.mp3'

The module should create or obtain an Audio instance and use it when the logo is clicked.

Conceptually:

click
 ↓
audio.currentTime = 0
 ↓
audio.play()

Resetting currentTime allows repeated strikes.

Audio protection

Rapid clicking shouldn't create an absolute mess of overlapping sounds.

You should therefore have a configurable cooldown.

For example:

audioCooldown: 120

Meaning:

click
 ↓
sound plays
 ↓
additional clicks during cooldown ignored
 ↓
cooldown expires
 ↓
next click allowed

Don't make the cooldown too long. The user should still be able to click the object repeatedly and hear individual strikes.

Browser audio restrictions

The sound should only be triggered from the user's click interaction.

Do NOT attempt:

page load
 ↓
automatically play metal sound

Browsers commonly restrict autoplay with sound.

A click is already a user gesture, so it is the appropriate moment to play the audio.

Pointer lighting algorithm

A useful model is:

logo bounding rectangle
        +
cursor coordinates
        ↓
relative cursor position
        ↓
normalized X/Y
        ↓
CSS custom properties

For example:

--logo-light-x
--logo-light-y
--logo-tilt-x
--logo-tilt-y

Then CSS can use those variables to control the appearance.

This keeps the actual rendering in CSS.

The JS essentially becomes:

cursor position
      ↓
calculate interaction
      ↓
update CSS variables

rather than manually manipulating dozens of visual properties.

Hover state

You can distinguish:

pointer far away
pointer approaching
pointer over logo
pointer leaving

The transition should be smooth.

For example:

far
 ↓
normal metallic appearance

near
 ↓
subtle highlight

over
 ↓
stronger specular response

But don't turn the logo into a glowing button.

Click animation state

Maintain a tiny internal state:

logoState = {
    hovered: false,
    pressed: false,
    interactionStrength: 0
}

On click:

pressed = true
    ↓
CSS impact animation
    ↓
pressed = false

The CSS animation can handle the actual timing.

JS shouldn't run a complicated physics simulation just to make the logo vibrate.

Accessibility

The logo is interactive, so it should be keyboard accessible.

If it's implemented as a div, give it:

tabindex="0"
role="button"

and allow:

Enter
Space

to trigger the same interaction as clicking.

Even better, if appropriate to the HTML structure, use a <button> and style it to look like the logo.

Then accessibility comes much more naturally.

Reduced motion

Respect:

prefers-reduced-motion

If the user has requested reduced motion:

disable entrance movement
disable hover movement
disable impact animation

The logo should still exist and remain usable.

The click sound can remain available because reduced motion is about animation, not necessarily audio.

Initialization

The public API should be:

initLogoAnimation()

Usage:

const logoController = initLogoAnimation();

It should find:

#personal-logo

or whatever selector you establish in the HTML.

If the element doesn't exist:

don't crash
don't create random UI
return safely

This is especially useful while the rest of the site is still being developed.

Public controller

I'd expose:

initLogoAnimation()

and optionally:

controller.destroy()
controller.getState()

destroy() should:

remove pointer listeners
remove keyboard listeners
remove click listener
cancel active timers/animations
release audio references
restore modified CSS variables
